// A full-screen terminal interface, bound to termbox2.
//
// There is no `dependencies` block because termbox2 depends on nothing but the C library and the
// POSIX terminal interface named below. The header itself is carried in this package.
//
// This is the block a *consumer* writes to reach this package, and resolution for it is built
// (`design/packages.md` §§ 3-6): a `sysl build` fetches the coordinate, selects by MVS, checks what
// arrived against `sysl.sum`, and compiles it as more modules.
//
//     dependencies {
//       termbox2 { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/termbox2", version = "0.1.2" }
//     }
//
// The coordinate is identity rather than a URL, so it carries no `https://`, and the version is the
// tag `v0.1.2` on this repository. `build-lib` and `--lib` still work and are what the tests here
// use, but they are no longer the only way in.

// `name` is the package; the module a program imports is `sh.sysl.termbox2`, and the two are
// deliberately different. The module carries the `sh.sysl.` prefix so that no package claims a
// top-level word another one might want; the package name is the short one, because it names this
// repository rather than a namespace.

package {
  name    = "termbox2"
  version = "0.1.2"
}

// **`posix`, because a terminal is what this package is for.** termbox puts the terminal into raw
// mode with `tcsetattr`, asks its size with `ioctl`, waits on it with `select`, and hears about a
// resize through `sigaction` and a `pipe`. None of those is ISO C. Saying so here turns "this cannot
// work on a freestanding target" into one refusal before anything is compiled, rather than a link
// error naming `tcsetattr`.
//
// **`alloc`, because the screen is a heap allocation.** termbox holds two cell buffers sized to the
// terminal, and reallocates both on a resize; the input buffer grows the same way. Unlike monocypher,
// which allocates nothing and therefore names nothing, this is a library whose whole job needs a
// heap -- the size of a terminal is not known until it is asked.
//
// `posix` implies `os`, so that one is not written out.
//
// **What is NOT here is a `@link` directive**, and the absence is the point of vendoring: the
// implementation is compiled from the header carried in this package, so there is no external library
// for the linker to be given (`15 §8`). Compare qcbor, which names `m` because its numeric coercions
// reach `pow`.
requires {
  alloc = true
  posix = true
}
